      ],
      "text": "A rainbow is a spectacular optical phenomenon that occurs when sunlight interacts with water droplets suspended in the atmosphere, creating a vibrant arc of colors. This natural display is not caused by the sun itself changing color, but rather by the complex interplay of light physics involving refraction, reflection, and dispersion within tiny spherical water droplets. To understand how this occurs, one must trace the journey of a single ray of white sunlight as it enters a raindrop.\n\nThe process begins with **refraction**. As parallel rays of white sunlight approach a raindrop, they strike the front surface of the droplet. Because water is denser than air, the light slows down and bends as it enters the water. This bending of light is known as refraction. Crucially, different wavelengths of light within the white beam bend by slightly different amounts. Red light, having the longest wavelength, bends the least, while violet light, with the shortest wavelength, bends the most. This separation of white light into its constituent spectral colors is called **dispersion**.\n\nOnce the light is dispersed inside the droplet, it travels toward the back of the sphere. Upon reaching the inner surface, the light undergoes **internal reflection**. Specifically, the light reflects off the back of the droplet,",
